<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <AssemblyName>MgcbPluginGate</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>ShadowDusk.Validation.MgcbPluginGate</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Deliberately dependency-free (BCL only). This driver's whole job is to prove what a REAL
    consumer sees: it shells out to the real `dotnet mgcb` and the real ShadowDuskCLI binary and
    compares the resulting bytes. Referencing ShadowDusk.Compiler would let it compile in-process
    and quietly stop testing the thing under test.

    Building the plugin is NOT this project's job either - it uses whatever
    src/ShadowDusk.MgcbPlugin/bin/{Debug,Release}/net8.0 already holds, exactly like
    CliBinaryFixture does for the CLI, so the gate measures the build you are about to ship.
  -->

  <!--
    The SECOND MGCB arm (Phase 63 Area B, issue #203): the real dotnet-mgcb 3.8.5, which
    renumbered TargetPlatform (Web=12, DesktopVK=13, WindowsDX12=14, XboxSeries=15) and is
    where the plugin's member-keyed map silently built an OpenGL payload for /platform:DesktopVK.
    PackageDownload is NOT a dependency: nothing is referenced, it only puts the package in the
    NuGet cache (the same mechanism validation/ForwardCompat uses for its runtimes), where
    Program.LocateMgcb finds tools/net8.0/any/mgcb.dll. The tool-manifest arm (3.8.4.1, the
    original numbering) stays; both must pass.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageDownload Include="dotnet-mgcb" Version="[3.8.5]" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
